/* Datapath | Interview hub | expanded tool, analysis and statistics questions */
(function () {
  "use strict";
  const DP = window.Datapath;
  DP.interviewBank.add([
    {id:'excel-q06',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What changes when you copy a relative reference, and when would you lock a cell?',answer:'If I copy `B2` down one row, Excel changes it to `B3`. When I need a fixed input, like a tax rate in `$F$2`, I lock the reference so it does not move as I copy the formula.'},
    {id:'excel-q07',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'When would you use IF or IFS in a worksheet?',answer:'I use IF when I need one condition to return one result or another—for example, show “On target” when sales meet a goal. If there are several conditions to check in order, IFS can make the formula easier to read.'},
    {id:'excel-q08',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does IFERROR do, and when should you be careful with it?',answer:'I use IFERROR when I want to handle a known formula error with a useful result, such as “Not found” for a lookup. I avoid wrapping everything in IFERROR because it can hide a real problem that I should fix instead.'},
    {id:'excel-q09',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How would you count rows that meet two conditions?',answer:'I would use COUNTIFS and give it a range and criterion for each condition. For example, I could count orders for the North region that are also marked “Complete,” then check a few matching rows to confirm the criteria.'},
    {id:'excel-q10',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you use conditional formatting in a report?',answer:'I use conditional formatting to make an important pattern easier to spot, such as overdue dates or values below a target. I keep the rule meaningful and check the underlying values so the color is not the only way to understand the result.'},
    {id:'excel-q11',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When might you use INDEX and MATCH instead of VLOOKUP?',answer:'I use INDEX and MATCH when I need more control over the lookup—for example, when the return column is to the left of the lookup column or the table layout might change. In newer Excel versions, XLOOKUP can often make the same task simpler.'},
    {id:'excel-q12',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you write a SUMIFS formula with more than one condition?',answer:'I give SUMIFS the values to add, then pair each criteria range with its condition. For example, to total completed orders for the North region, I would sum the amount column and apply both the status and region conditions.'},
    {id:'excel-q13',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are dynamic array functions useful for?',answer:'They let a formula return a range of results that spills into nearby cells. I might use FILTER to show only orders above a chosen amount, then make sure the spill area is clear and the source data is correct.'},
    {id:'excel-q14',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you handle dates that look like dates but will not sort correctly?',answer:'I check whether Excel has stored them as real date values or as text. If they are text, I convert them using a method that matches the source format, then sort a few examples and confirm the order is chronological.'},
    {id:'excel-q15',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you clean extra spaces from text in Excel?',answer:'I usually start with TRIM to remove leading, trailing and repeated ordinary spaces. If the data came from a web export, I check for non-breaking spaces too, because TRIM alone may not remove those.'},
    {id:'excel-q16',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between sorting and filtering?',answer:'Sorting changes the order in which rows appear; filtering temporarily hides rows that do not match a condition. Before either one, I make sure the whole table is selected so values stay with the correct record.'},
    {id:'excel-q17',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'When would you use data validation?',answer:'I use data validation to guide what people can enter, such as allowing only dates in a range or choosing a department from a list. It reduces common entry mistakes, although I still check existing data because validation does not clean old values.'},
    {id:'excel-q18',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why convert a range into an Excel Table?',answer:'I convert a range into a Table when the data will grow or be reused. The Table expands with new rows, carries formulas and formatting down, and gives formulas readable column names instead of fragile cell ranges.'},
    {id:'excel-q19',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Power Query useful for in Excel?',answer:'I use Power Query to import and repeat data-preparation steps, such as changing types, removing unwanted columns or combining files. Once the steps are set up, I can refresh them on new data and review any source changes that break the process.'},
    {id:'excel-q20',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you append monthly files with the same columns?',answer:'I would use Power Query to load the files from a folder and append their rows, after checking that the columns mean the same thing in each file. I would validate the combined row count and date coverage before using it in a report.'},
    {id:'excel-q21',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How is Power Query different from a worksheet formula?',answer:'A worksheet formula usually calculates a result in cells, while Power Query records preparation steps that I can rerun when I refresh the source. I choose based on the task: formulas are handy for worksheet calculations, and Power Query is useful for repeatable imports and reshaping.'},
    {id:'excel-q22',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'A PivotTable total is unchanged after you update the source. What do you do?',answer:'I would refresh the PivotTable and check that its source range includes the updated rows. If new rows are added regularly, I would use an Excel Table as the source so I do not have to keep extending a fixed range.'},
    {id:'excel-q23',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you choose a chart for a simple business report?',answer:'I start with the question. I use a line chart for change over time, bars to compare categories and a scatter plot to examine how two numeric measures relate. Then I keep labels clear and remove decoration that makes the comparison harder.'},
    {id:'excel-q24',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you investigate a formula that returns the wrong total?',answer:'I would trace the formula, check the range and criteria, and inspect a few source rows by hand. I would also look for numbers stored as text, hidden filters or references that moved when the formula was copied.'},
    {id:'excel-q25',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What would you check when an Excel date calculation gives an unexpected result?',answer:'I would check that both cells contain valid date values and that the formula uses the intended units. I would inspect the cell format too, because a correct date serial can look confusing if it is displayed as a number or with the wrong date format.'},
    {id:'excel-q26',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you find why XLOOKUP returns “Not found” for a code you can see?',answer:'I would compare the lookup value with the source value for hidden spaces, different data types or slightly different characters. I would also check that I selected the right lookup and return ranges and that both ranges line up row by row.'},
    {id:'excel-q27',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you recognize numbers stored as text?',answer:'I look for warning indicators, inconsistent alignment or formulas that ignore values I expected to count. I confirm by testing a few cells, then convert them using a controlled method and recheck totals rather than assuming every text value should become a number.'},
    {id:'excel-q28',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'A large workbook is slow. How would you make it more manageable?',answer:'I would first find what is causing the delay—large formulas, volatile functions, excess formatting or repeated calculations. Then I would simplify or limit the expensive work and test the workbook again, checking that the outputs have not changed.'},
    {id:'excel-q29',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you audit a workbook before sharing it?',answer:'I check the source, formulas, filters, date range and totals, then spot-check important results against the underlying records. I also look for broken links, hidden sheets and unclear labels, and make sure the report explains its scope.'},
    {id:'excel-q30',area:'Excel',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How would you keep a shared workbook safe from accidental edits?',answer:'I would keep an untouched source copy, separate input areas from formulas and protect cells where it helps prevent mistakes. I would also explain which fields users should update and test the workbook as a normal user before sharing it.'},

    {id:'sql-q06',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does DISTINCT do in a query?',answer:'I use DISTINCT when I want unique combinations of the selected columns. I do not add it automatically to fix duplicate-looking results, because duplicates may reveal a join or grain issue that I should understand first.'},
    {id:'sql-q07',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How are COUNT(*) and COUNT(column) different?',answer:'COUNT(*) counts rows. COUNT(column) counts only rows where that column is not NULL, so the numbers can differ when the column has missing values.'},
    {id:'sql-q08',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How does SQL handle NULL values in a comparison?',answer:'A comparison with NULL does not behave like a comparison with an ordinary value. I use `IS NULL` or `IS NOT NULL` to test for it, and check how NULLs affect aggregates and joins in the query.'},
    {id:'sql-q09',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'When would you use CASE in SQL?',answer:'I use CASE to return a value based on conditions, such as grouping orders into “small” and “large” bands. I make the conditions clear and ordered, then include an ELSE result for values that do not match.'},
    {id:'sql-q10',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you group results by month in SQL?',answer:'I convert or truncate the date to the month using the functions supported by that database, then group on that month and aggregate the measure. I check whether the month label includes the year so January from different years does not get combined.'},
    {id:'sql-q11',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When is a correlated subquery useful?',answer:'I use a correlated subquery when the inner query needs a value from the current outer row, for example checking whether a customer has an order. For a large dataset, I would compare the query plan with a join or EXISTS approach rather than assume one is faster.'},
    {id:'sql-q12',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL?',answer:'UNION combines results and removes duplicate rows; UNION ALL keeps every row. I use UNION ALL when I want to retain all records and do not need the extra duplicate-removal work.'},
    {id:'sql-q13',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you return the top three products within each region?',answer:'I would rank products within each region using a window function such as ROW_NUMBER or RANK, ordered by the chosen measure, then filter to the top three. I would clarify how ties should be handled before picking the ranking function.'},
    {id:'sql-q14',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'When would you choose RANK instead of ROW_NUMBER?',answer:'I use ROW_NUMBER when every row needs a unique sequence, even if values tie. I use RANK when tied values should share a rank and the next rank should have a gap, then explain that choice in the query.'},
    {id:'sql-q15',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What can LAG or LEAD help you calculate?',answer:'I use LAG or LEAD to compare a row with a previous or next row in an ordered group—for example, this month’s sales against last month’s. I define the partition and ordering carefully so the comparison is meaningful.'},
    {id:'sql-q16',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a primary key?',answer:'A primary key is the column or set of columns I rely on to identify each row uniquely. Before treating a field as a key, I check that it has no duplicates or missing values.'},
    {id:'sql-q17',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does a foreign key represent?',answer:'A foreign key links a row to a related row in another table, such as an order pointing to a customer. It helps express the relationship, and I still check the data for unmatched keys when analyzing real tables.'},
    {id:'sql-q18',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you find duplicate customer records in SQL?',answer:'I would first agree on which fields define the same customer, then group by those fields and count rows. I would inspect the matching records before changing anything, because similar names can belong to different people.'},
    {id:'sql-q19',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why might you use EXISTS instead of joining a table?',answer:'I use EXISTS when I only need to know whether a related row is present, not return its columns. It expresses that check clearly; I would still review the query plan and make sure the matching condition is correct.'},
    {id:'sql-q20',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of a subquery?',answer:'A subquery lets me use the result of one query inside another—for example, filter customers whose total spending is above an average. If the logic gets hard to follow, I may rewrite it as a CTE.'},
    {id:'sql-q21',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you limit the rows returned by a query?',answer:'I use the row-limiting syntax supported by the database, such as LIMIT or TOP. If I need a meaningful “top” result, I include an ORDER BY; otherwise, the chosen rows may not be predictable.'},
    {id:'sql-q22',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why does the order of operations matter in a SQL query?',answer:'SQL logically filters rows before grouping, then filters groups, and finally sorts the result. Knowing that order helps me put each condition in the right place and understand why an aggregate cannot usually be filtered in WHERE.'},
    {id:'sql-q23',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between DELETE and TRUNCATE?',answer:'DELETE removes rows and can usually target them with a condition. TRUNCATE is generally used to clear a whole table more directly, but its behavior and transaction rules depend on the database, so I check the platform before using it.'},
    {id:'sql-q24',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do DDL and DML differ?',answer:'DDL changes database structures, such as creating or altering a table. DML works with the data inside those structures, for example inserting or updating rows.'},
    {id:'sql-q25',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does an index do, and what is its trade-off?',answer:'An index can help the database find rows faster for certain filters or joins. It uses storage and can make writes slower because the index also has to be maintained, so I would add one based on the workload and query plan.'},
    {id:'sql-q26',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you use an execution plan when a query is slow?',answer:'I use the execution plan to see how the database is reading and joining the data, and where it estimates or spends the most work. I look for issues such as scans over large tables or unexpected row counts, then test a focused change and compare run time and results.'},
    {id:'sql-q27',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why can filtering on a joined table change a LEFT JOIN result?',answer:'If I put a condition on the right-side table in WHERE, rows with no match may be removed because that value is NULL. I check whether the condition belongs in the join or whether the request really intends to exclude those unmatched rows.'},
    {id:'sql-q28',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you avoid dividing by zero in a SQL calculation?',answer:'I handle the denominator explicitly, for example with NULLIF or a CASE expression supported by the database. Then I decide whether a zero denominator should produce NULL, a label or another agreed result rather than silently presenting a misleading zero.'},
    {id:'sql-q29',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a transaction, and when would you use one?',answer:'A transaction groups related database changes so they can succeed or fail together. I would use one when a set of updates must remain consistent, and I would commit only after validating the work or roll it back if something went wrong.'},
    {id:'sql-q30',area:'SQL',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you make an analytical SQL query easier for someone else to review?',answer:'I use clear aliases, consistent formatting and CTEs when they make the steps easier to follow. I state the grain and join logic clearly, avoid unexplained `SELECT *`, and include checks such as expected row counts or totals.'},

    {id:'python-q06',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between a Pandas Series and a DataFrame?',answer:'I think of a Series as one labeled column and a DataFrame as a table made up of columns. In my sales analysis, I used DataFrames to work with the rows and columns, then selected Series when I needed one field.'},
    {id:'python-q07',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'When would you use `.loc` and `.iloc` in Pandas?',answer:'I use `.loc` when I want to select by labels or conditions, and `.iloc` when I want positions such as the first five rows. I check the index so I do not confuse a label with a row number.'},
    {id:'python-q08',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you read a CSV file with Pandas?',answer:'I usually start with `read_csv`, then inspect the first rows, column names and data types. If the file is large or has known issues, I specify only the columns I need and set important types or parsing rules explicitly.'},
    {id:'python-q09',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you combine two DataFrames with a shared key?',answer:'I use `merge` and specify the key and join type so the result matches the question. Before trusting it, I check whether the key is unique where expected and compare row counts, because a many-to-many match can multiply rows.'},
    {id:'python-q10',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does `groupby` do in Pandas?',answer:'I use `groupby` to split rows into groups and calculate summaries for each one. For example, I could group sales by product and sum revenue, then compare the output with the source totals.'},
    {id:'python-q11',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When would you use `apply`, and when would you avoid it?',answer:'I use `apply` when a transformation genuinely needs a custom function that is not easy to express another way. For simple column calculations, I first try a vectorized expression because it is often clearer and faster.'},
    {id:'python-q12',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you find duplicate rows in a DataFrame?',answer:'I use `duplicated` to flag repeated rows or repeated values in chosen columns. I decide which columns define a duplicate first, then inspect the flagged records before dropping anything.'},
    {id:'python-q13',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you handle a column with inconsistent text capitalization?',answer:'I standardize the text with a consistent case, such as lowercasing it, after checking whether capitalization carries meaning. I also trim spaces and review the distinct values to make sure I did not combine categories that should stay separate.'},
    {id:'python-q14',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a Python exception?',answer:'An exception signals that something went wrong while the code was running, such as trying to convert invalid text to a number. I handle expected errors where I can respond usefully, and avoid catching every error in a way that hides the real issue.'},
    {id:'python-q15',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you make a data-cleaning script easier to reuse?',answer:'I would put repeated steps into focused functions, pass in the file or settings they need, and return a clear result. I would keep the cleaning rules visible and test the functions on a small known example before using them on a full dataset.'},
    {id:'python-q16',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a list comprehension?',answer:'A list comprehension is a compact way to create a list from an iterable, sometimes applying a condition or transformation. I use it when it stays readable, and switch to a regular loop when the logic needs several steps.'},
    {id:'python-q17',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is NumPy useful for in data analysis?',answer:'I use NumPy for efficient arrays and numerical operations, especially when I need to work with many values at once. Pandas is often more convenient for labeled tables, and it uses NumPy arrays as part of its data model.'},
    {id:'python-q18',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you convert a column to a numeric type safely in Pandas?',answer:'I use a controlled conversion such as `to_numeric` and inspect which values fail. I do not turn failures into missing values without counting and reviewing them, because that could hide a source-data problem.'},
    {id:'python-q19',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a virtual environment, and why use one?',answer:'A virtual environment keeps a project’s installed packages separate from other projects. I use one so I can record the dependencies a script needs and avoid changing another project’s setup.'},
    {id:'python-q20',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How would you parse dates in a Pandas DataFrame?',answer:'I use `to_datetime` with the known format when possible, then inspect any values that fail. I also check the time zone and date range so the parsed column matches the reporting question.'},
    {id:'python-q21',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you test a small Python function?',answer:'I would give the function a few known inputs and check that the outputs match what I expect. I include ordinary cases and edge cases such as empty or missing values, so a later change does not quietly break the result.'},
    {id:'python-q22',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Why is it useful to set a random seed in an analysis?',answer:'If an analysis uses randomness, setting a seed helps me reproduce the same sample or split while I debug and compare results. I still record the method and software environment, because the seed alone does not make every workflow reproducible.'},
    {id:'python-q23',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a chained assignment warning in Pandas?',answer:'It can appear when I modify a view that may or may not be connected to the original DataFrame. I make the intended operation explicit—often by using `.loc` or creating a copy—then verify that the expected rows changed.'},
    {id:'python-q24',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you make a Matplotlib chart easier to understand?',answer:'I give it a descriptive title, label both axes with units where relevant, and choose a chart type that fits the comparison. I remove clutter and check that the scale does not exaggerate the pattern.'},
    {id:'python-q25',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'When might you choose Seaborn over Matplotlib?',answer:'I often use Seaborn when I want a statistical chart with sensible defaults, such as a grouped distribution or relationship plot. Matplotlib gives me more direct control, and Seaborn charts can also be customized with Matplotlib.'},
    {id:'python-q26',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you avoid accidentally changing the original DataFrame?',answer:'When I need a separate working version, I make a copy and keep the source unchanged. I also use clear variable names and compare row counts or key summaries so I can tell which steps changed the data.'},
    {id:'python-q27',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What would you do if a CSV file has more columns than fit in memory?',answer:'I would first select only the fields and rows needed for the analysis. If memory is still a problem, I would process chunks or query the data through a suitable database, then validate that the combined results match the expected totals.'},
    {id:'python-q28',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you keep a notebook analysis understandable?',answer:'I organize the notebook into clear steps, explain important decisions in short markdown notes, and keep outputs current. I restart and run it from the top before sharing so the results do not depend on hidden execution order.'},
    {id:'python-q29',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you save a cleaned dataset for someone else to use?',answer:'I would save it in a format suited to the next step, include clear column names and types, and document the cleaning rules. Before handing it over, I would reload the saved file and check the row count and key summaries.'},
    {id:'python-q30',area:'Python',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you choose between a loop and a vectorized operation?',answer:'I use a loop when each step depends on the result of the previous one or when it makes the logic clearer. For operations that apply independently to a whole column or array, I usually try a vectorized form and test that it handles missing values and boundaries correctly.'},

    {id:'powerbi-q05',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What steps would you take to prepare data in Power Query?',answer:'I start by checking the source and its grain, then remove fields I do not need, set the right data types and handle obvious quality issues. I keep the steps understandable and refresh the query to confirm they still work on updated data.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q06',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What are Applied Steps in Power Query?',answer:'Applied Steps show the transformations Power Query will repeat in order when the data refreshes. I give the steps clear names where useful and check the result after changes to the source.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q07',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why do relationships matter in a Power BI model?',answer:'Relationships let filters move between related tables, so a date or product selection can affect the fact-table measures. I check the keys, cardinality and filter direction because an incorrect relationship can produce totals that look plausible but are wrong.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q08',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is cardinality in a Power BI relationship?',answer:'Cardinality describes how many rows on each side can match—for example, one product in a dimension table can relate to many sales rows. I make sure the relationship matches the real data rather than forcing a one-to-many assumption.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q09',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does CALCULATE do in DAX?',answer:'I use CALCULATE to evaluate an expression under a modified filter context. For example, I can calculate sales for one product group while respecting the other report filters, then test the measure in a visual to confirm the context behaves as intended.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q10',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How are row context and filter context different in DAX?',answer:'Row context means DAX is working with a current row, as in a calculated column or an iterator. Filter context is the set of filters affecting a calculation, such as selections in a visual. Understanding the two helps me explain why a measure behaves differently from a column.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q11',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why use variables in a DAX measure?',answer:'I use variables to name intermediate results, avoid repeating the same expression and make a measure easier to read. I check that the variable is evaluated in the context I intend, especially when filters change.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q12',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you calculate year-to-date sales in Power BI?',answer:'I use a proper date table and a DAX time-intelligence pattern such as TOTALYTD, after confirming the relationship and calendar range. I test it across year boundaries and compare the result with a source-level check.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q13',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a date table, and why is it useful?',answer:'A date table gives the model one consistent row for each date and useful fields such as month, quarter and year. I mark and relate it appropriately so time-based measures and filters behave consistently.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q14',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is row-level security?',answer:'Row-level security restricts which rows a user can see based on their role or identity. I define and test the roles with representative users, because a report that looks correct for me may show a different slice to someone else.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q15',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between a report and a dashboard in Power BI Service?',answer:'A report can contain multiple interactive pages built from a semantic model. A dashboard in the service is a single canvas of pinned tiles, often bringing visuals together. I choose based on whether users need deeper exploration or a concise monitoring view.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q16',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a Power BI workspace used for?',answer:'A workspace is where a team develops and manages content such as reports and semantic models. I use roles and permissions deliberately, then distribute finished content through the appropriate app or access method.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q17',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When would you use drill-through?',answer:'I use drill-through when someone needs to move from a summary to a detail page for a selected item, such as one region or product. I make the destination clear and test that the right filters carry across.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q18',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a bookmark useful for?',answer:'A bookmark can save a report view, including selected filters and visual state, so users can switch between useful views or open a guided story. I test the bookmark after edits because saved display settings can become outdated.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q19',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you choose visuals for a Power BI report?',answer:'I start with the comparison the user needs: trends over time, category comparisons, proportions or a detailed lookup. I choose the simplest visual that answers it and make labels, units and sorting clear.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q20',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you make a Power BI report more accessible?',answer:'I use readable contrast and text, descriptive titles, consistent layouts and more than color alone to communicate status. I also check keyboard navigation and alt text where supported, and ask users to try the report.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q21',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How would you investigate a Power BI report that loads slowly?',answer:'I would identify whether the delay is in the model, query, measure or visual. Then I would reduce unnecessary columns and visuals, check relationships and DAX, and use the available performance tools to test targeted changes without changing the meaning of the report.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q22',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is query folding in Power Query?',answer:'Query folding means Power Query can translate supported transformations into a query that runs at the source. I check whether folding is preserved when performance matters, because some steps or connectors can prevent it.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q23',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When might you use incremental refresh?',answer:'I consider incremental refresh for a large table where older periods change rarely and only recent data needs frequent updates. I check licensing and service requirements, define the date filters correctly and validate that historical data is included.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q24',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What should you check before scheduling a dataset refresh?',answer:'I check the source credentials, gateway needs, refresh limits and whether the query completes successfully in the service. I also confirm the schedule matches when the source data is ready and communicate what users should expect if a refresh fails.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q25',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does a slicer do?',answer:'A slicer is a visible filter control that lets a report user select values such as a year or region. I label it clearly and check which visuals it affects so its behavior is not surprising.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q26',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you avoid confusing many-to-many relationships?',answer:'I first inspect the data grain and keys to see why both sides contain repeated values. Where possible, I model a bridge or dimension table with clear relationships, then test totals under different filters instead of relying on an ambiguous relationship.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q27',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When would you use Power Query rather than DAX for a transformation?',answer:'I use Power Query for shaping data before it enters the model, such as cleaning text or combining files. I use DAX for calculations that need to respond to report filter context. I keep the model simple and avoid doing the same work in both places.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q28',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you explain a KPI card to a stakeholder?',answer:'I would state what the number measures, the time period and the comparison behind it. For example, a card showing 4.6% conversion should make clear which visitors and completed actions form the numerator and denominator, and whether that is up or down from a meaningful baseline.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q29',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What would you do if a DAX measure works in one visual but not another?',answer:'I would compare the filters and grouping in both visuals and test the measure in a small table with the relevant fields. Then I would inspect the model relationships and the measure’s context transitions before changing the DAX.'},
    {id:'powerbi-q30',area:'Power BI',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you decide whether to use Import or DirectQuery?',answer:'I compare the data volume, freshness requirement, source performance and model features available for each mode. Import is often responsive for report users but needs refreshes; DirectQuery can query the source as users interact, so I test its performance and limitations with the actual workload.'},

    {id:'analysis-q06',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you decide what metric to use for a business question?',answer:'I start with the decision the team needs to make and ask what observable change would matter. Then I define the population, numerator, denominator and time window, and check that the data can measure it consistently.'},
    {id:'analysis-q07',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between a metric and a KPI?',answer:'A metric is any measure I can calculate, while a KPI is a measure selected to track progress toward an important goal. I would explain why the KPI matters and what action someone might take if it changes.'},
    {id:'analysis-q08',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you decide what level of detail a report should show?',answer:'I match the detail to the user’s decision. A leader may need a monthly summary, while an analyst investigating a change may need daily or transaction-level detail. I make sure the report’s grain is clear and does not encourage invalid comparisons.'},
    {id:'analysis-q09',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does data quality mean to you?',answer:'I look at whether the data is complete enough, valid, consistent and suitable for the question—not whether it is perfect in every possible way. I document issues that could change the conclusion and agree with the data owner on how to handle them.'},
    {id:'analysis-q10',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you decide whether an outlier is an error?',answer:'I check the original record, the field’s valid range and whether similar values occur elsewhere. I do not remove an unusual value just because it looks extreme; I confirm whether it is a real event or a data problem and report the treatment.'},
    {id:'analysis-q11',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a cohort analysis?',answer:'I group people or events by a shared starting point, such as the month a customer first joined, and compare how their behavior changes over time. I make the cohort definition and follow-up window clear so the comparison is fair.'},
    {id:'analysis-q12',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How would you define customer retention?',answer:'I would first ask what “retained” means for this business—for example, a customer making another purchase within a set period. Then I would define the starting cohort, follow-up window and denominator, and check that the same rule is used across periods.'},
    {id:'analysis-q13',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Why segment an analysis?',answer:'I segment when an overall result might hide meaningful differences, such as performance by region or customer type. I choose groups that relate to the question and check sample sizes so I do not overstate a pattern from a very small segment.'},
    {id:'analysis-q14',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a data dictionary?',answer:'A data dictionary records what fields mean, their types, allowed values and relevant business rules. I use it to interpret columns consistently and ask the data owner when a definition is unclear.'},
    {id:'analysis-q15',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you prioritize analysis when a request is vague?',answer:'I ask what decision is waiting, what deadline matters and what information would change that decision. I then agree on a small useful first result, define what is out of scope and share the assumptions before doing detailed work.'},
    {id:'analysis-q16',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you explain a finding to someone who is not technical?',answer:'I start with the business question and give the result in everyday language, using a simple chart or comparison if it helps. I avoid tool jargon, explain the important caveat and check that the person understands what action the finding does—or does not—support.'},
    {id:'analysis-q17',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you distinguish a trend from a one-time fluctuation?',answer:'I look across a suitable time period, compare with a relevant baseline and check whether the pattern repeats across groups or data sources. I also consider seasonality and changes in measurement before describing it as a trend.'},
    {id:'analysis-q18',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why is a denominator important when reporting a percentage?',answer:'A percentage is only meaningful when I know what is in the denominator. I state the population and period, and I check whether it changed between comparisons—for example, a higher conversion rate may come from a different mix of visitors.'},
    {id:'analysis-q19',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you check that a dashboard answers its intended question?',answer:'I compare each visual with the decision it is meant to support and ask whether a user can find the relevant comparison quickly. I test filters and totals against the source, then ask a representative user to try common tasks.'},
    {id:'analysis-q20',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What would you include in a data-analysis handover?',answer:'I would include the question, sources, definitions, cleaning decisions, calculations and key limitations. I would also leave a clear output and explain how to refresh or reproduce it, so someone else can check the work.'},
    {id:'analysis-q21',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you handle a request for a metric that is not available in the data?',answer:'I would explain what information is missing and whether there is a reasonable proxy. If I use one, I would label it clearly and describe what it cannot measure; otherwise, I would suggest what data needs to be collected.'},
    {id:'analysis-q22',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can survivorship bias affect an analysis?',answer:'It can happen when I only analyze entities that made it to the end of a process, while excluding those that dropped out. I would check who is missing from the dataset and how that could change the conclusion before generalizing it.'},
    {id:'analysis-q23',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you handle conflicting definitions of the same metric?',answer:'I would compare the definitions and examples with the teams using them, then agree on which one fits the decision. If both are useful for different purposes, I would name them distinctly and document the calculation so they are not confused.'},
    {id:'analysis-q24',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between a population and a sample?',answer:'The population is the full group I want to understand, while the sample is the subset I actually observe. I check how the sample was selected before using it to describe the broader population.'},
    {id:'analysis-q25',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How would you evaluate a change after a process was introduced?',answer:'I would define the outcome and compare an appropriate period before and after, while checking for other changes that happened at the same time. If possible, I would use a comparison group or experimental design, and I would separate a measured association from a causal claim.'},
    {id:'analysis-q26',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you choose a useful baseline for comparison?',answer:'I choose a baseline that represents a fair comparison for the question—for example, the same season last year or a comparable group. I explain why it is relevant and check that the metric definition and data coverage match.'},
    {id:'analysis-q27',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does data grain mean, and why do you check it?',answer:'The grain tells me what one row represents, such as one order or one order item. I check it before joining or aggregating because combining tables at different grains can duplicate values or produce incorrect totals.'},
    {id:'analysis-q28',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you decide whether a difference is practically important?',answer:'I look at the size of the difference in the business context, not only whether it is statistically detectable. I compare it with a threshold or cost that matters to the decision and explain uncertainty around the estimate.'},
    {id:'analysis-q29',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you protect sensitive information in an analysis?',answer:'I use only the data needed for the question, follow the organization’s access rules and avoid including personal details in reports unless there is a clear reason. I check sharing permissions and aggregate or mask information when that still supports the decision.'},
    {id:'analysis-q30',area:'Data Analysis',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you make an analysis reproducible?',answer:'I keep the source and transformation steps clear, record definitions and assumptions, and save code or query versions where relevant. I also rerun the workflow from the beginning and check key counts and totals before sharing it.'},

    {id:'statistics-q06',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'When is the mean a useful summary?',answer:'I use the mean when the values are reasonably balanced and the average itself answers the question. If a few extreme values pull it away from what is typical, I also look at the median and the shape of the data.'},
    {id:'statistics-q07',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does standard deviation tell you?',answer:'It gives me a sense of how spread out numeric values are around their mean. I interpret it alongside the distribution and units, because the same standard deviation can mean different things in different contexts.'},
    {id:'statistics-q08',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How would you explain the interquartile range?',answer:'The interquartile range is the distance from the 25th percentile to the 75th percentile. I use it to describe the spread of the middle half of the data, and it is less affected by extreme values than the full range.'},
    {id:'statistics-q09',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a skewed distribution?',answer:'A distribution is skewed when one tail stretches farther than the other. I check for skew because it can make the mean less representative, and it can affect which summary or method is appropriate.'},
    {id:'statistics-q10',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between standard deviation and standard error?',answer:'Standard deviation describes variation among individual observations. Standard error describes uncertainty in a sample estimate, such as a sample mean. I make clear which one I am showing because they answer different questions.'},
    {id:'statistics-q11',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a sampling distribution?',answer:'It is the distribution of a statistic across repeated samples drawn in the same way. I use that idea to understand how much a sample estimate might vary and why larger, well-designed samples often give more stable estimates.'},
    {id:'statistics-q12',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What factors affect the width of a confidence interval?',answer:'The interval gets wider when the estimate is more variable or the sample is smaller, and narrower when there is more information, under the method’s assumptions. I would state the confidence level and method when I report it.'},
    {id:'statistics-q13',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the null hypothesis?',answer:'The null hypothesis is the reference statement used in a statistical test, often saying there is no difference or association. I compare the data with that model, but I do not treat the null as automatically true in the real world.'},
    {id:'statistics-q14',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a Type I error?',answer:'A Type I error is rejecting the null hypothesis when it is true. In plain terms, the test flags an effect that is not really there under the assumptions. I consider the cost of that mistake when choosing a testing approach.'},
    {id:'statistics-q15',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a Type II error?',answer:'A Type II error is failing to reject the null when a real effect exists. In practice, that means a test may miss a meaningful change, which is one reason sample size and measurement quality matter.'},
    {id:'statistics-q16',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does statistical power mean?',answer:'Power is the chance that a test will detect an effect of a specified size when that effect exists, under the assumptions. I would plan around an effect that matters in practice, not just choose a sample size because it is convenient.'},
    {id:'statistics-q17',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why does sample size matter?',answer:'A larger sample can reduce random uncertainty, but it cannot automatically fix biased sampling or poor measurement. I consider both how precise the estimate needs to be and whether the sample represents the group I care about.'},
    {id:'statistics-q18',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How would you explain a percentile?',answer:'A percentile tells me the value at or below which a given share of observations falls. For example, the 90th percentile of delivery time is a point that about 90% of observed deliveries meet or beat, depending on the exact convention.'},
    {id:'statistics-q19',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a confounding variable?',answer:'A confounder is a factor related to both the exposure and the outcome that can make an association misleading. I think about plausible confounders when comparing groups and avoid claiming causation from a simple correlation.'},
    {id:'statistics-q20',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between correlation and covariance?',answer:'Both describe how two variables vary together, but covariance depends on the variables’ units and is harder to compare across pairs. Correlation standardizes that relationship to a scale from minus one to one, but neither measure proves causation.'},
    {id:'statistics-q21',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a practical limitation of using Pearson correlation?',answer:'Pearson correlation measures a linear relationship and can be strongly affected by outliers. I would plot the data, check for non-linear patterns and consider another measure if the assumptions do not fit.'},
    {id:'statistics-q22',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a confidence level?',answer:'The confidence level describes how often an interval-building method would capture the true value over repeated samples, under its assumptions. It is not a statement that the method is correct for any particular biased dataset.'},
    {id:'statistics-q23',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Why can multiple hypothesis tests create a problem?',answer:'When I run many tests, the chance of at least one false positive can increase. I would define primary comparisons in advance and consider an appropriate adjustment or validation approach, while reporting how many tests were run.'},
    {id:'statistics-q24',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why should an A/B test use random assignment?',answer:'Random assignment helps make the groups comparable on average, including factors I did not measure. I would still check that assignment worked, that users saw the intended experience and that analysis follows the original assignment.'},
    {id:'statistics-q25',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Why should you not stop an A/B test as soon as a result looks significant?',answer:'If I keep checking and stop the moment the result crosses a threshold, the false-positive rate can be higher than planned. I would set a sample-size or duration plan in advance or use a valid sequential-testing method.'},
    {id:'statistics-q26',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a margin of error?',answer:'It describes a range around a sample estimate under a stated method and assumptions. I would report the estimate, the interval and the sampling context rather than present the margin as a guarantee against bias.'},
    {id:'statistics-q27',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How do you decide whether to use the mean or median?',answer:'I look at the question and the distribution. For fairly balanced data, the mean can summarize the center well; for skewed data or strong outliers, the median may better describe a typical observation. I often show both when the difference matters.'},
    {id:'statistics-q28',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is Simpson’s paradox?',answer:'It is when a trend appears in separate groups but reverses or changes after those groups are combined, often because the group sizes differ. I check important segments as well as the overall result and explain which view fits the decision.'},
    {id:'statistics-q29',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a bootstrap method?',answer:'Bootstrapping repeatedly resamples the observed data with replacement to estimate how a statistic varies. It can be useful when a simple formula is not convenient, but it still depends on the sample representing the process I want to understand.'},
    {id:'statistics-q30',area:'Statistics',kind:'interview',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How would you explain the difference between statistical and practical significance?',answer:'Statistical significance is about how compatible the data are with a null model under the test assumptions. Practical significance asks whether the size of the effect matters for the decision. I report the effect estimate and uncertainty so the audience can consider both.'}
  ]);
})();
